behavior:
  expected: >
    No screen draws a coloured rim border. RADIAL's centre disc shows the
    active RPM band's colour unconditionally (no shift-state colour, no
    flash). The seven call sites of _draw_shift_border() are removed, the
    method and _get_shift_cue() are removed, and the Palette fields that
    existed only to supply them (shift_border_caution, shift_border_normal,
    shift_border_down, shift_centre_dark, band_centres_lit) are removed
    from DAY_PALETTE and NIGHT_PALETTE and from the Palette dataclass.
    band_centres is retained — it is still the centre disc's source
    unconditionally.
  actual: >
    _draw_shift_border() draws a 12 px (or 5 px, on the setup fallback)
    ring at the outer edge of the circular viewport on every screen.
    On RADIAL, the ring's colour and the centre disc's flash both come
    from _get_shift_cue(), keyed to RPM against the caution and torque
    thresholds. On every other screen the ring is drawn in the screen's
    own background colour, so it currently produces no visible effect
    there — the call is present but cosmetically inert.
  impact: >
    Operator-facing: removes a rim indicator found distracting on RADIAL
    (the only screen where it was visible) and drops the upshift flash
    and the downshift/normal border-colour distinction, which have no
    replacement (confirmed with reporter — the distinction is not to be
    preserved on another channel). Six other screens lose an inert draw
    call with no visible change. Three test files stub
    _draw_shift_border directly and will need updating once the method
    no longer exists: tests/test_connect_error_classification.py,
    tests/test_bluetooth_reset.py, tests/test_disconnected_screen.py.
  workaround: "None required — cosmetic/UX change, not a defect."

environment:
